 ],
 "literal": "Gentle, sweet, desirable, worthy things / 'these we will not speak', even if I say, those things, you / do you forget? May you live, my heart! Many things together, / of the beautiful mango tree, pollen-abiding flower, / bees falling and moving, in the seaside grove, / the cool sea's lord, after seeing?",
 "translation": "O my heart, may you live! Even when I tell you, 'Let us not speak of these gentle, sweet, desirable, and worthy things,' do you forget that advice? The moment you see the lord of the cool seashore, where bees fall upon and swarm around the pollen-rich flowers of the lovely mango tree in the seaside grove, you forget everything at once!",
 "modern": "என் நெஞ்சமே, நீ வாழ்க! 'மென்மையான, இனிய, விரும்பத்தக்க, தகுதியான இந்த வார்த்தைகளை நாம் அவரிடம் பேசக் கூடாது' என்று நான் உனக்குச் சொன்னாலும், அழகிய மாமரத்துப் பூக்களின் தாதுக்களை விரும்பி வண்டுகள் வீழ்ந்து ஒலிக்கும் நெய்தல் நிலக் கானலையும், குளிர்ந்த கடலையும் உடைய தலைவனைக் கண்ட உடனே, நீ அந்தப் பலவற்றையும் மறந்துவிடுகிறாயோ?",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine to her own heart in the Neytal (coastal) landscape. She gently chides her heart for its lack of resolve. She had resolved not to speak sweet, gentle, and loving words to the hero, perhaps to show her anger or distress over his delay. However, the moment she sees the lord of the cool seashore, her heart immediately forgets all its resolutions and rushes to him. The imagery of the bees falling upon the pollen-rich flowers of the mango tree in the seaside grove mirrors her heart's natural, uncontrollable attraction to the hero. The key words are 'நெஞ்சே' (O heart), 'மறத்தியோ' (do you forget?), and 'சேர்ப்பன்' (lord of the shore).",
